From eb2b2721994a3ca1b02c846c16d60e0424044e7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:36:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/14] PS1-RAM step 2: table strings in sections of their own
 (+29,376 B)

With -fdata-sections every function's string literals have a section of
their own, but all the literals a file's tables point to share one
(.rodata.str1.4), and the overlay planner places whole sections. One
resident pointer into it (a scene's name, two tab labels in .sdata) kept
every table string of the file in resident RAM: 40 KB in 61 files.

ps1/tools/strsec.py is a compiler launcher (DOOMO_STRING_SECTIONS, default
ON): it compiles to assembly, gives each $LC<n> of .rodata.str1.4 its own
mergeable section, and assembles with the command's machine flags. Without
the rewrite the two steps give the one-step object byte for byte
(STRSEC_VERIFY=1 checks it per file: all 315 objects of the game disc pass).
ovl_plan.py then keeps resident exactly the strings resident code or data
reach; the linker still merges equal strings and now drops unread ones.

STR_WHOLE: the story and house groups set the slot's size, so their objects
keep their table strings together as before (2.2 + 1.4 KB more in them
would grow the slot by 2 KB each).

Resident merged strings 108,748 -> 77,948 B. Groups: library 90 -> 96 KB,
creator 150 -> 154, cutscene 116 -> 119, combat 121 -> 123, free play 117
(slot 131,072 B, unchanged). No source file changes.

Game disc with the boot FMV: 87,744 -> 117,120 B below the stack top.

test_tools.py: the rewrite on assembly text and on real compiles, and the
planner's placement of split strings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RLcGbHBa2kju7ynbaJLHT6
